  {
   "n": 25,
   "type": "MSQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66",
   "html": "<p>\n\tWhich of the following statements is/are correct?</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "<p>\n\tIn a min-heap, the next largest element of any element can be found is Ο(log <em>n</em>) time.</p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "<p>\n\tIn a BST, finding the next smallest element to given element can be done is Ο(<em>n</em>) time.</p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "<p>\n\tBinary insertion sorting requires Ο(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>) total operations.</p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "<p>\n\tAny <em>n</em>-node unbalanced tree can be balanced using Ο(log <em>n</em>) rotations.</p>"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "B, A, C, D",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 2,
    "neg": 0.66
   },
   "solution": "<p>\n\t(b)<br />\n\t(a) False: A min-heap can provide next largest element, we need to do a linear search through the heap&rsquo;s array it will take &Omicron;(<em>n</em>) time.<br />\n\t(b) True: Finding the next smallest element, the predecessor, may require traveling down the height of the tree, making the running time &Omicron;(<em>n</em>).<br />\n\t(c) False: Binary insertion sorting improve the time it take to find right position for next element being inserted. It may still take &Omicron;(<em>n</em>) time to perform swaps necessary to shift it into place.<br />\n\tThis results is an &Omicron;(n<sup>2</sup>) running time, the same as that of insertion sort.<br />\n\t(d) False: The worst case unbalanced tree is a list, and balancing it require &Omicron;(<em>n</em>) rotations.</p>"
  },
